          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/bloom-filter.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Bloom Filter\\ndescription: A probabilistic data structure that tests set membership with a tunable false-positive rate and no false negatives.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-05T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/DataStructure\\n  - concept/Probabilistic\\n  - pattern/SpaceEfficiency\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source provides clear technical definition; no corroboration yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nA bloom filter is a probabilistic set-membership data structure. It efficiently answers membership queries using a bit array and k independent hash functions.\\n\\n## Characteristics\\n\\n**Query behaviour:**\\n- Answers \\u0022definitely not present\\u0022 with certainty (no false negatives)\\n- Answers \\u0022possibly present\\u0022 with a tunable false-positive rate\\n\\n**Limitations:**\\n- Cannot enumerate its members\\n- In its basic form, does not support deletion \\u2014 removing a set bit could break membership testing for other elements that hash to the same positions\\n\\nThe false-positive rate is configurable by adjusting the size of the bit array and the number of hash functions used.\\n\\n## Use cases\\n\\nBloom filters are valuable in scenarios where space efficiency is paramount and false positives are acceptable trade-offs, such as:\\n- Cache lookups\\n- Duplicate detection\\n- Database query optimization\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://log-newest-first-placement/sample-01\\n\u0022}"
